                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Stereo Microphone</h2>
                    <p>Blue&#8217;s Yeti is a 16-bit USB microphone with an unusual array of three small-diaphragm capsules.</p><p><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/blue/yeti-capsules.jpg" width="120" alt="BLUE Yeti Capsule Array" class="thumb shaft" />One capsule faces forward. The other two form a 90&deg; XY-stereo array pointing 180&deg; from the front capsule. </p><p>Presumably all three capsules produce a cardioid pattern. The front of the mic (single capsule) would then provide a cardioid pattern; the rear (capsule pair) would provide an XY-stereo pattern. Summing the stereo signal to mono cardioid would allow it to be combined to form omni (back-to-back cardioid in phase) or figure-8 (back-to-back cardioid out of phase) patterns.</p><p>Blue&#8217;s literature gives a capsule diameter of 14mm. No other details about the capsules are available at this time.</p><p>The microphone body has five switches:</p><ol><li>Headphone volume</li><li>Pattern selection - cardioid, stereo, omni, figure-of-8</li><li>Mute - instantly silence the mic output</li><li>Mic gain</li></ol><p>The Yeti is the world&#8217;s first THX Certified Microphone.</p><blockquote><p><cite>Matthew Chrispen, THX</cite></p><p>THX testing and certification ensure [that] the fidelity of the recording always remains true to its source,</p></blockquote><p>The mini phone jack on the microphone can be used for zero-latency monitoring of the mic&#8217;s signal, or for monitoring tracks played back from the computer. Note, however, that the mic provides no means of adjusting the relative volumes of these two sources.</p><p>The mic&#8217;s published specs quote a frequency response of 20 - 20,000 Hz, but the response graphs for the mic show that both the low and high-frequency -3dB points happen well inside the edges of the graph. The ranges noted in the specs table below represent our best read of the published graphs.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Blue-Microphones/Yeti">Blue Microphones Yeti</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2009.</p>
